00:07:45.640 --> 00:07:47.530
is that something that
developers can just

00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:48.320
use directly?

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:49.903
BRENT SHAFFER: That's
a good question.

00:07:49.903 --> 00:07:52.830
So we do support some
of the larger frameworks

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:55.071
out there, like for instance,
WordPress runs really

00:07:55.071 --> 00:07:56.820
well on App Edition
standard because we've

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:00.180
put a lot of effort into making
that an easy process to get up

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:01.050
and running.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:02.830
We also have some documentation.

00:08:02.830 --> 00:08:05.420
We're getting Symfony
up and running.

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:08.460
So there are a lot of
restrictions in App Engine

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:12.910
classic because it's a sandbox
environment, so anybody that's

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:14.780
using another
framework out there,

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:16.910
there's quite a few
with PHP, it's not

00:08:16.910 --> 00:08:20.380
like the Ruby community where
they've all kind of agreed

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:22.040
on one or two
frameworks, there's just

00:08:22.040 --> 00:08:23.490
a ton of PHP frameworks.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:27.730
So yeah, we don't have tutorials
out there for every single one,

00:08:27.730 --> 00:08:31.660
but I think it is worth
trying to deploy in App Engine

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:33.142
standard.

00:08:33.142 --> 00:08:34.600
We have a lot of
good documentation

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:40.000
on what is and isn't supported
for App Engine standard PHP

00:08:40.000 --> 00:08:40.539
runtime.

00:08:40.539 --> 00:08:44.360
So for instance, if you know
you need a certain extension,

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:46.630
you should check
the documentation

00:08:46.630 --> 00:08:49.140
and if it's not available
in App Engine standard,

00:08:49.140 --> 00:08:52.710
then you want to run that
framework in App Engine

00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:53.760
flexible environment.

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:55.551
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Can
you give some examples

00:08:55.551 --> 00:08:57.017
of things that might not work?

00:08:57.017 --> 00:08:58.600
TERRY RYAN: Well for
the longest time,

00:08:58.600 --> 00:09:01.400
ImageMagick wasn't supported
but we now support that,

00:09:01.400 --> 00:09:04.360
so that's gone away
and it's not a problem.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:08.050
We did, in the last big
rev of PHP on App Engine,

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:13.370
fix a lot of the most requested
things that were missing.

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:15.430
I don't know off the
top of my head something

00:09:15.430 --> 00:09:18.430
that would be-- it
would be something

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:23.190
that-- a not widely used tool.

00:09:23.190 --> 00:09:25.190
CHRIS BROADFOOT: So the
majority of applications

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:26.089
will run just fine?

00:09:26.089 --> 00:09:27.630
BRENT SHAFFER: Well
yeah, and there's

00:09:27.630 --> 00:09:33.530
a lot of frameworks that require
the file system by default

00:09:33.530 --> 00:09:34.220
out of the box.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:35.850
So I guess it's
more like, it's not

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:37.850
a question of whether it
will run in App Engine,

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:40.230
it's a question of
how much work would

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.240
be required to get
it to the point

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.390
where it would
run on App Engine.

00:09:45.390 --> 00:09:47.610
And that varies per framework.

00:09:47.610 --> 00:09:51.160
So for instance, the WordPress
and the Symfony tutorials

00:09:51.160 --> 00:09:53.060
we have, we've gone
ahead and we've

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:55.300
made sure we take care of
that kind of stuff for you.

00:09:55.300 --> 00:09:57.490
But for Laravel,
for CakePHP, we're

00:09:57.490 --> 00:09:59.600
still working on getting
those solutions out there

00:09:59.600 --> 00:10:01.310
so you don't have
to do it yourselves.

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:04.280
And for somebody that's
new to the platform, that

00:10:04.280 --> 00:10:07.260
deploys one of these
frameworks, that

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:08.950
might be a frustrating
process at first,

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:11.829
trying to tweak the knobs of
the framework to get it to run.

00:10:11.829 --> 00:10:14.120
TERRY RYAN: I was going to
say, the big thing that I've

00:10:14.120 --> 00:10:17.030
run into is, as you point out,
that they need the file system.

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:19.180
And I find, they
need the file system

00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:20.830
to write a configuration.

00:10:20.830 --> 00:10:24.410
However, once that
configuration file is written,

00:10:24.410 --> 00:10:26.880
it doesn't actively edit
that configuration every time

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.530
the app runs, it's
like a one time thing.

00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:32.670
And so if you can get them
set correctly in development

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:35.990
and pushed to production,
you typically are OK.

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.354
That's been my experience
with the couple of frameworks

00:10:38.354 --> 00:10:39.520
that I've experimented with.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:41.520
And not some of the
bigger ones, like Lumen

00:10:41.520 --> 00:10:44.600
and those types of frameworks.

00:10:44.600 --> 00:10:47.140
BRENT SHAFFER: Yeah, so I've
found that the frameworks that

00:10:47.140 --> 00:10:50.500
generate configuration for
their production environment,

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:53.200
it's very difficult to get
them to generate correctly

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:54.400
in a dev environment.

00:10:54.400 --> 00:10:58.580
And so what I've typically done
is use Google Cloud Storage

00:10:58.580 --> 00:10:59.770
as a file system.

00:10:59.770 --> 00:11:03.590
And because you have a stream
wrapper, so instead of saying,

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:08.709
these files are going to be
written to cache/templates,

00:11:08.709 --> 00:11:10.750
you just say, these files
are going to be written

00:11:10.750 --> 00:11:15.720
to gs://bucketname/cache.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:19.010
And then it all will
actually function as you

00:11:19.010 --> 00:11:20.390
would expect it to function.

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:22.890
CHRIS BROADFOOT: And so I guess
that would be a shared cache

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:25.900
as well, across someone's-- So
what's the best way for someone

00:11:25.900 --> 00:11:28.942
to get started and to use
Cloud storage for that?

00:11:28.942 --> 00:11:30.400
BRENT SHAFFER: Well
they could look

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:33.690
at the Symfony starter project.

00:11:33.690 --> 00:11:37.250
We have, the stream wrappers
being used as a shared

00:11:37.250 --> 00:11:39.560
cache in that project.

00:11:39.560 --> 00:11:44.480
The WordPress, there's
a get help repository

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:47.510
that we have all of these
framework samples in.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:50.380
There's a WordPress one
that does the same thing.

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:52.734
So yeah, there's a couple
examples of us doing that.

00:11:52.734 --> 00:11:53.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:11:53.650 --> 00:11:57.060
So of these restrictions
that we're talking about,

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:01.020
they are related to a App
Engine standard environment?

00:12:01.020 --> 00:12:04.970
Do we have any problem
running your PHP

00:12:04.970 --> 00:12:06.699
on flexible environment?

00:12:06.699 --> 00:12:08.740
BRENT SHAFFER: Yeah, so
we've been working really

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:12.040
hard on the App Engine
flexible runtime

00:12:12.040 --> 00:12:14.780
for PHP and that's something
that builds in docker

00:12:14.780 --> 00:12:17.350
and so it's really portable
and it's really great.

00:12:17.350 --> 00:12:19.840
You can run it in
Kubernetes cluster

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:21.560
and we have some
pretty cool docs

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:24.850
on how to do that, also
running microservices

00:12:24.850 --> 00:12:27.730
and multiple services
that are communicating

00:12:27.730 --> 00:12:31.260
with each other in Kubernetes
cluster and also in App Engine

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:31.810
flexible.

00:12:31.810 --> 00:12:34.220
Because since App
Engine flexible runs

00:12:34.220 --> 00:12:37.790
docker container images, they're
all compatible, which is nice.

00:12:37.790 --> 00:12:41.979
That runtime is on GitHub
and it's in the repository

00:12:41.979 --> 00:12:43.270
googlecloudplatform/php-docker.

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:49.900
But it is almost an alpha.

00:12:49.900 --> 00:12:54.130
So we have a lot of docs,
we have a lot of stuff

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:56.850
and we're putting a lot of
time into making it good,

00:12:56.850 --> 00:13:02.370
but I think it's important to
note it's not quite in alpha.

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:04.290
TERRY RYAN: It's almost alpha.

00:13:04.290 --> 00:13:05.090
BRENT SHAFFER: Maybe by
the time this goes live,

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:05.970
it will be an alpha.

00:13:05.970 --> 00:13:06.900
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:06.900 --> 00:13:09.330
Yeah, no, this is
coming out next week.

00:13:09.330 --> 00:13:11.200
So I don't know
how fast you are,

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:14.330
but that is a challenge now.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:14.830
Cool.

00:13:14.830 --> 00:13:17.820
So you mentioned Kubernetes.

00:13:17.820 --> 00:13:20.587
That is actually something
that our audience loves.

00:13:20.587 --> 00:13:22.920
We keep on talking about
Kubernetes and Google Container

00:13:22.920 --> 00:13:27.150
Engine basically every
episode, more or less.

00:13:27.150 --> 00:13:28.580
How is the experience?

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:32.450
I know a lot of people that
come to PHP because of the LAMP

00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:33.130
stack.

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:36.440
That P in the lamp
is PHP or Python,

00:13:36.440 --> 00:13:39.850
apparently, according to
what I just read, surprising.

00:13:39.850 --> 00:13:43.044
But yeah, so if I come
from LAMP stack, what

00:13:43.044 --> 00:13:44.210
is the best place to run it?

00:13:44.210 --> 00:13:46.550
Could it be standard App Engine?

00:13:46.550 --> 00:13:48.950
Could it be flexible App Engine?

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:51.227
Could it be Container
Engine, Compute Engine?

00:13:51.227 --> 00:13:53.310
TERRY RYAN: I think the
answer is going to depend.

00:13:53.310 --> 00:13:59.540
So if you're running
a LAMP stack box,

00:13:59.540 --> 00:14:03.290
the first thing you got to say
is, like, that's really bad.

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:05.600
Don't do that anymore.

00:14:05.600 --> 00:14:08.452
But for whatever
reason, your friend

00:14:08.452 --> 00:14:10.160
set it up, not you,
your friend set it up

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:11.750
and now you are
responsible for it.

00:14:11.750 --> 00:14:17.800
So we have, in Compute
Engine, LAMP stack images

00:14:17.800 --> 00:14:18.810
in Cloud Launcher.

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:20.600
So you want to get
started on LAMP?

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:24.830
Boom, you just run it on Cloud
Launcher, and you're there.

00:14:24.830 --> 00:14:26.270
What is the best way to do it?

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:27.320
It's going to depend.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:30.350
If you are moving towards
a containerized workload,

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:33.760
then I would definitely tell
you to look at Kubernetes

00:14:33.760 --> 00:14:37.000
and Container Engine and
splitting it up that way,

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:39.680
with the caveat that
the M is going to be

00:14:39.680 --> 00:14:42.370
the problematic part of that.

00:14:42.370 --> 00:14:47.090
Kubernetes and containers
and persistent database,

00:14:47.090 --> 00:14:49.100
it's not always a good combo.

00:14:49.100 --> 00:14:51.740
And then you're trading in
one disaster for another.

00:14:51.740 --> 00:14:57.050
So I would say, look
at getting-- hey,

00:14:57.050 --> 00:14:59.470
you have a lot of
options, you can

00:14:59.470 --> 00:15:03.235
go App Engine, either standard
or flexible, running Cloud SQL,

00:15:03.235 --> 00:15:05.465
and that solves a lot
of problems for you.

00:15:05.465 --> 00:15:09.170
You can go with
Kubernetes and Cloud SQL

00:15:09.170 --> 00:15:13.490
or you can run your full My
SQL on Kubernetes as a pet,

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:15.900
I would not recommend
that, I would say,

00:15:15.900 --> 00:15:18.500
go without Cloud
SQL for the M part.

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:19.860
And then, it's going to depend.

00:15:19.860 --> 00:15:21.220
Are you moving containers?

00:15:21.220 --> 00:15:25.320
Then the Container
Engine would probably

00:15:25.320 --> 00:15:27.010
be the best place
for you to look.

00:15:27.010 --> 00:15:30.880
Are you anticipating
incredible amounts of scale

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:33.440
or incredibly variable
amounts of scale

00:15:33.440 --> 00:15:35.350
where App Engine really shines?

00:15:35.350 --> 00:15:37.220
Well, then obviously,
I'd go App Engine.

00:15:40.530 --> 00:15:43.594
Let's go with the full
flow diagram here.

00:15:43.594 --> 00:15:45.010
If you're in that
scenario and you

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:47.360
need to run bits that won't
run on App Engine standard

00:15:47.360 --> 00:15:49.420
for whatever reason,
then flexible

00:15:49.420 --> 00:15:51.630
is probably what you want
to do with the caveat

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:55.296
that it is currently pre-alpha.

00:15:55.296 --> 00:15:56.670
We don't even have
a name for it.

00:15:56.670 --> 00:16:00.004
So I mean, I would go through
the whole explanation,

00:16:00.004 --> 00:16:01.920
and that's how I'd talk
to a customer about it

00:16:01.920 --> 00:16:02.870
if they were interested.

00:16:02.870 --> 00:16:05.495
BRENT SHAFFER: Yeah, I think if
you have an existing app that's

00:16:05.495 --> 00:16:11.080
pretty sizable, using a
GC instance, just a VM

00:16:11.080 --> 00:16:15.357
and possibly using
the LAMP deployment

00:16:15.357 --> 00:16:17.940
is going to be the easiest way
to get your app up and running.

00:16:17.940 --> 00:16:21.290
But if you're coming at it
with a fresh application,

00:16:21.290 --> 00:16:23.790
I would definitely encourage
containerizing first

00:16:23.790 --> 00:16:30.250
with flexible, because it's
such an easy move from flex

00:16:30.250 --> 00:16:31.410
to Kubernetes.

00:16:31.410 --> 00:16:35.950
And so if you think you may
require scale eventually,

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:37.502
it's a really nice
way to go, not

00:16:37.502 --> 00:16:38.960
to mention just
the portability you

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:41.190
get from containers
and images, is really

00:16:41.190 --> 00:16:43.244
nice as a software engineer.

00:16:43.244 --> 00:16:44.910
TERRY RYAN: And one
of the things that's

00:16:44.910 --> 00:16:47.326
come up when I've talked about
Kubernetes is that idea of,

00:16:47.326 --> 00:16:52.090
so you have the LAMP
stack, and the code is all

00:16:52.090 --> 00:16:54.690
looking at itself, looking
at everything as level host.

00:16:54.690 --> 00:16:56.523
But one of the great
things about Kubernetes

00:16:56.523 --> 00:16:59.610
is that you can run multiple
containers in the same pod

00:16:59.610 --> 00:17:01.230
as offering a local host.

00:17:01.230 --> 00:17:04.359
And that is, there's a nice
bridging stepping stone

00:17:04.359 --> 00:17:08.589
towards getting to a
better place with your app,

00:17:08.589 --> 00:17:12.869
is moving into Kubernetes and
taking advantage of the fact

00:17:12.869 --> 00:17:14.560
that you containerize
it but also leave

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:17.420
the code non-containerized.

00:17:17.420 --> 00:17:20.550
Not ideal, but most of these
are bridging solutions anyway.

00:17:20.550 --> 00:17:22.550
CHRIS BROADFOOT: What's
the best way for someone

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:25.440
to use something like
Engine X as a proxy?

00:17:25.440 --> 00:17:26.940
BRENT SHAFFER: I
was actually trying

00:17:26.940 --> 00:17:28.670
to work this in
the conversation,

00:17:28.670 --> 00:17:34.410
so we don't have a LAMP stack,
but we do have more or less

00:17:34.410 --> 00:17:35.640
a LEMP stack, right?

00:17:35.640 --> 00:17:39.700
It's on Flex.

00:17:39.700 --> 00:17:42.610
Yeah, so Flex uses Engine X.

00:17:42.610 --> 00:17:45.762
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Francesc
and I are trying to decode.

00:17:45.762 --> 00:17:49.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Are you
just talking about the names?

00:17:49.250 --> 00:17:51.020
TERRY RYAN: Engine
X instead of Apache.

00:17:51.020 --> 00:17:52.436
CHRIS BROADFOOT:
Yeah, it's the E.

00:17:52.436 --> 00:17:54.235
FRANCESC CAMPOY: How
do you pronounce that?

00:17:54.235 --> 00:17:56.610
BRENT SHAFFER: I realize the
nuances in the pronunciation

00:17:56.610 --> 00:17:58.034
might have been lost.

00:17:58.034 --> 00:18:00.640
"lahmp" and "leemp,"
I don't know.

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:04.150
Yeah, so we use Engine
X. And then the M,

00:18:04.150 --> 00:18:08.100
Cloud SQL works exactly
like a MySQL instance

00:18:08.100 --> 00:18:10.500
so it integrates perfectly.

00:18:10.500 --> 00:18:14.350
TERRY RYAN: Just
to disambiguize,

00:18:14.350 --> 00:18:17.320
that's on the App
Engine flexible runtime.

00:18:17.320 --> 00:18:19.310
When I said earlier
about Cloud Launcher,

00:18:19.310 --> 00:18:23.000
we do have full on LAMP stack
boxes in Cloud Launcher.

00:18:23.000 --> 00:18:24.625
Just getting all out
acronyms in a row.

00:18:24.625 --> 00:18:26.458
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I'm
actually curious about,

00:18:26.458 --> 00:18:27.980
when you have the
Cloud Launcher,

00:18:27.980 --> 00:18:32.500
do we also have things that
depend directly on Cloud SQL,

00:18:32.500 --> 00:18:36.170
or do they all start
one instance, which

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:38.160
is like the SQL instance?

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:39.910
TERRY RYAN: As far as
I know, none of them

00:18:39.910 --> 00:18:42.207
point to Cloud SQL as
their SQL solution.

00:18:42.207 --> 00:18:43.290
It doesn't mean you can't.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:44.980
It just means that out
of the box, they don't.

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:45.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Just need
to configure it a little bit.

00:18:45.930 --> 00:18:46.860
Cool.

00:18:46.860 --> 00:18:49.930
Yeah, I think that is something
that most people, regardless

00:18:49.930 --> 00:18:52.980
of if they do PHP
or not, they should

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:58.070
consider is running your own SQL
databases, consider Cloud SQL.

00:18:58.070 --> 00:19:02.670
Version two is so much better,
it is very fast and it scales.

00:19:02.670 --> 00:19:06.544
You can actually make the
disk bigger, which is magic.

00:19:06.544 --> 00:19:07.210
That is amazing.

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:10.170
You don't need to migrate
and have weird moments where

00:19:10.170 --> 00:19:11.920
you need to make your
database read-only,

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:13.610
so you can migrate
to something else.

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:14.642
It just works.

00:19:14.642 --> 00:19:16.350
TERRY RYAN: It's easy
to set up replicas,

00:19:16.350 --> 00:19:19.260
it's easy to set up
Nearline backups.

00:19:19.260 --> 00:19:26.570
Cloud SQL V2 is magic, with the
caveat that it is not yet GA.

00:19:26.570 --> 00:19:28.140
BRENT SHAFFER: Pre-alpha.

00:19:28.140 --> 00:19:30.497
TERRY RYAN: It's still not GA.

00:19:30.497 --> 00:19:33.080
I mean, it's been running a long
time, and it's really stable,

00:19:33.080 --> 00:19:34.413
and it's all these great things.

00:19:34.413 --> 00:19:37.276
But if you are a company
or you need an SLA,

00:19:37.276 --> 00:19:38.400
we don't have an SLA on it.

00:19:38.400 --> 00:19:41.240
So it's one thing,
but if you look,

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.890
it's very clear from
the UI flow, right?

00:19:43.890 --> 00:19:47.790
You go to it, and
it's like, try V2,

00:19:47.790 --> 00:19:50.246
or there's this
Cloud One down there.

00:19:50.246 --> 00:19:51.062
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Down
there at the bottom.

00:19:51.062 --> 00:19:51.853
You need to scroll.

00:19:51.853 --> 00:19:52.467
Yeah.

00:19:52.467 --> 00:19:54.050
TERRY RYAN: So we
definitely, I think,

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:56.180
want to push people
to V2, but make

00:19:56.180 --> 00:19:58.887
sure that people understand
that the SLA is not there yet.

00:19:58.887 --> 00:20:00.470
BRENT SHAFFER: And
how about Postgres?

00:20:00.470 --> 00:20:04.020
Is that a big thing
in the PHP community?

00:20:04.020 --> 00:20:05.682
CHRIS BROADFOOT: No,
I don't think so.

00:20:05.682 --> 00:20:07.390
TERRY RYAN: Bigger in
the Ruby community.

00:20:07.390 --> 00:20:08.990
BRENT SHAFFER: And we do
have click to deploy Postgres

00:20:08.990 --> 00:20:09.640
instances.

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:15.380
And PDO, the database
abstraction library for PHP,

00:20:15.380 --> 00:20:17.790
supports Postgres, too,
so it's definitely there

00:20:17.790 --> 00:20:19.240
if people want to use it.

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:20.410
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Great.

00:20:20.410 --> 00:20:24.020
And so other than running
apps, what else is new,

00:20:24.020 --> 00:20:26.030
in terms of PHP, on Cloud?

00:20:26.030 --> 00:20:27.800
BRENT SHAFFER: Well,
so we are working

00:20:27.800 --> 00:20:31.100
on a pretty nice
library that people can

00:20:31.100 --> 00:20:33.590
use to access the Cloud APIs.

00:20:33.590 --> 00:20:38.260
And again, one of the great
things about the PHP space

00:20:38.260 --> 00:20:40.540
is it's rapidly developing
and it's getting better.

00:20:40.540 --> 00:20:43.165
Unfortunately, that means that
a lot of the stuff we're talking

00:20:43.165 --> 00:20:47.340
about is still in pre-alpha
slash not GA states,

00:20:47.340 --> 00:20:50.550
but one of those things
is our Cloud library.

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:54.290
And we have a lot of
really nice APIs, really

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:57.890
nice wrappers for
APIs, that you can

00:20:57.890 --> 00:21:06.270
use to manage Pub/Sub,
BigQuery, data store, everything

00:21:06.270 --> 00:21:06.770
pretty much.

00:21:06.770 --> 00:21:08.994
TERRY RYAN: And the new
library, like the vision API.

00:21:08.994 --> 00:21:10.660
BRENT SHAFFER: Vision
is in there, yeah.

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:14.650
Natural language
API is in there.

00:21:14.650 --> 00:21:18.700
I mean, pretty much every cloud
API has a wrapper in PHP now,

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:20.869
and they're all really,
really nice to use

00:21:20.869 --> 00:21:23.410
and really easy to understand
compared to the previous stuff.

00:21:23.410 --> 00:21:26.214
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So are
those wrappers wrapping--

00:21:26.214 --> 00:21:27.380
which API are they wrapping?

00:21:27.380 --> 00:21:29.752
Are they wrapping the
REST API or the GRPC API?

00:21:29.752 --> 00:21:31.210
BRENT SHAFFER:
Yeah, good question.

00:21:31.210 --> 00:21:33.020
So they're wrapping
the REST API right now,

00:21:33.020 --> 00:21:36.600
but GRPC support is
definitely planned.

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:39.700
It's scheduled for before
the end of the year,

00:21:39.700 --> 00:21:41.000
so it should have both.

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:44.990
And then that will be able to
multiplex on one or the other,

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:47.946
depending on if you have the
GRPC extension installed.

00:21:47.946 --> 00:21:49.820
CHRIS BROADFOOT: And
speaking of GRPC, what's

00:21:49.820 --> 00:21:51.690
the state of that in PHP?

00:21:51.690 --> 00:21:53.690
BRENT SHAFFER: Yeah,
so it's supported.

00:21:53.690 --> 00:21:56.860
Again, to Google
investing in PHP,

00:21:56.860 --> 00:22:01.500
you can use PECL, which is
the PHP Extension Manager.

00:22:01.500 --> 00:22:03.530
So if you do PECL
installed GRPC,

00:22:03.530 --> 00:22:06.500
it will compile the C extension.

00:22:06.500 --> 00:22:12.780
And then once you have that,
you enable that C extension,

00:22:12.780 --> 00:22:14.660
and then you can use GRPC.

00:22:14.660 --> 00:22:18.329
TERRY RYAN: And that's on
PHP on Compute Engine, right?

00:22:18.329 --> 00:22:20.620
BRENT SHAFFER: You can do
that in Compute Engine, yeah.

00:22:20.620 --> 00:22:22.030
TERRY RYAN: Can you do
it on App Engine as well?

00:22:22.030 --> 00:22:24.330
I haven't tried it yet,
so I was just curious.

00:22:24.330 --> 00:22:25.913
BRENT SHAFFER: So
you could definitely

00:22:25.913 --> 00:22:27.250
do it with a custom runtime.

00:22:27.250 --> 00:22:33.130
We are going to build GRPC
into the supported runtime

00:22:33.130 --> 00:22:34.604
at some point, for sure.

00:22:34.604 --> 00:22:35.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:22:35.520 --> 00:22:37.544
So we are kind of
running out of time.

00:22:37.544 --> 00:22:39.210
Is there any other
topic that you'd like

00:22:39.210 --> 00:22:40.674
to discuss before we finish?

00:22:40.674 --> 00:22:41.840
BRENT SHAFFER: So I had one.

00:22:41.840 --> 00:22:43.631
I just wanted to mention,
one of the things

00:22:43.631 --> 00:22:46.570
that's really nice about
using the runtimes that we're

00:22:46.570 --> 00:22:49.940
trying to provide
is we're trying

00:22:49.940 --> 00:22:55.980
to tackle the misconception that
PHP is not a secure language.

00:22:55.980 --> 00:23:00.300
So we're spending a lot of
time making the runtime secure

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:02.400
by default. And
that means, we've

00:23:02.400 --> 00:23:05.580
done things like we've patched
core PHP functions that

00:23:05.580 --> 00:23:10.270
might have some really unsafe
operations and properties.

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:14.140
One instance of that is,
there's like the par string

00:23:14.140 --> 00:23:17.190
function that people use
to parse query strings.

00:23:17.190 --> 00:23:19.570
It can be used
improperly in a way that

00:23:19.570 --> 00:23:23.500
would override global variables,
and then thus be a really,

00:23:23.500 --> 00:23:26.080
really large threat factor.

00:23:26.080 --> 00:23:29.390
And so we've patched
PHP to not allow that.

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:32.760
There's also
security extensions.

00:23:32.760 --> 00:23:35.000
One is called
SUHOSIN that we use.

00:23:35.000 --> 00:23:37.720
And that does the same
thing, of black listing a lot

00:23:37.720 --> 00:23:42.820
of the functions that are
traditionally abused in PHP,

00:23:42.820 --> 00:23:45.350
and you can still use them.

00:23:45.350 --> 00:23:47.840
You can white list them
by setting an environment

00:23:47.840 --> 00:23:51.750
variable, but they are secured.

00:23:51.750 --> 00:23:53.490
They are locked down by default.

00:23:53.490 --> 00:23:57.860
So there's a couple other
things we do in that same vein.

00:23:57.860 --> 00:24:00.860
One of them kind of
being using Engine X

00:24:00.860 --> 00:24:04.910
and trying to do the things
that maybe aren't as largely

00:24:04.910 --> 00:24:08.170
accepted in the PHP community
as they are in other language

00:24:08.170 --> 00:24:08.910
communities.

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:12.120
And so we're trying to bring
those to PHP developers

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:17.120
without them having to
opt in or whatever else.

00:24:17.120 --> 00:24:18.155
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:24:18.155 --> 00:24:20.030
What about you, Terry,
is there anything else

00:24:20.030 --> 00:24:21.140
that you would like to mention?

00:24:21.140 --> 00:24:22.640
TERRY RYAN: Well,
the only think I'd

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:26.159
mention is that Bernstein does
a lot of work on the APIs.

00:24:26.159 --> 00:24:27.700
And one of things
I really appreciate

00:24:27.700 --> 00:24:29.660
about them is that they're
very clearly written

00:24:29.660 --> 00:24:33.890
by people that write PHP
and not like, oh, here's

00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:35.766
the Python
implementation in PHP.

00:24:35.766 --> 00:24:37.140
That's all on
GitHub, and I don't

00:24:37.140 --> 00:24:44.500
know how accepting of outside
input are we with them.

00:24:44.500 --> 00:24:47.060
BRENT SHAFFER: Oh, definitely.

00:24:47.060 --> 00:24:49.619
Any contributions, we're like
super, super excited about.

00:24:49.619 --> 00:24:51.410
TERRY RYAN: Yeah, so
if anybody's out there

00:24:51.410 --> 00:24:53.243
and they're fooling
around with these things

00:24:53.243 --> 00:24:56.550
and find either a--
not that there would

00:24:56.550 --> 00:24:58.610
be a problem, right, Brent?

00:24:58.610 --> 00:25:01.010
But you know, we're developers,
and we know how it goes.

00:25:01.010 --> 00:25:03.890
So we would definitely
welcome any contributions.

00:25:03.890 --> 00:25:07.330
BRENT SHAFFER: Yeah, and all
this stuff is at a early stage.

00:25:07.330 --> 00:25:11.860
So there's a couple of guys
