00:25:54.570 --> 00:25:58.600
Cool, so it's time to go with
our question of the week.

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:00.710
And the question of
the week this week

00:26:00.710 --> 00:26:04.380
is something that
I'm not a master on.

00:26:04.380 --> 00:26:09.600
As I've said many times, I'm
not a network expert at all.

00:26:09.600 --> 00:26:12.090
But it is something that
is pretty interesting.

00:26:12.090 --> 00:26:15.240
So we have the
HTTPS load balancer,

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:18.360
which is a load balancer
that works globally.

00:26:18.360 --> 00:26:22.160
So basically it does
routing, and it sends

00:26:22.160 --> 00:26:24.390
for whatever requests you get.

00:26:24.390 --> 00:26:26.400
For an HTTP request,
you'll be able to send it

00:26:26.400 --> 00:26:29.100
to the closest instance
group that you have running.

00:26:29.100 --> 00:26:32.810
So yeah, so if you send a
request coming from Barcelona,

00:26:32.810 --> 00:26:34.870
for instance, it will
go through the center

00:26:34.870 --> 00:26:36.620
that we have in Belgium.

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:38.590
But if you send it
from San Francisco,

00:26:38.590 --> 00:26:41.370
it could go to the new data
center in the West Coast.

00:26:41.370 --> 00:26:43.200
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Oregon, yeah.

00:26:43.200 --> 00:26:45.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: But
it has a restriction,

00:26:45.430 --> 00:26:47.338
which is, it doesn't
support WebSockets.

00:26:47.338 --> 00:26:48.296
CHRIS BROADFOOT: Right.

00:26:48.296 --> 00:26:51.250
And this is because
WebSockets aren't HTTP.

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:54.200
They are its own protocol,
which is somewhat

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:57.060
related to HTTP but
only in a little way.

00:26:57.060 --> 00:26:59.550
So it's kind of expected
that the HTTP load

00:26:59.550 --> 00:27:01.410
balancer doesn't work with it.

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:03.790
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
it is really not HTTP.

00:27:03.790 --> 00:27:09.440
It's more like TCP with
some HTTP sprinkled on top.

00:27:09.440 --> 00:27:11.730
So the question is, OK,
so since these are not

00:27:11.730 --> 00:27:15.112
supported, what do I do if
I want to have WebSockets

00:27:15.112 --> 00:27:16.890
on our global load balancer?

00:27:16.890 --> 00:27:18.390
CHRIS BROADFOOT:
Yeah, you certainly

00:27:18.390 --> 00:27:19.973
want some load
balancing, because it's

00:27:19.973 --> 00:27:22.010
quite an expensive protocol.

00:27:22.010 --> 00:27:25.570
So the solution is to
use the SSL load balancer

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:28.520
if you're using SSL
with your WebSockets,

00:27:28.520 --> 00:27:30.920
and I do recommend that.

00:27:30.920 --> 00:27:35.970
And if you're not, then you can
also use the TCP load balancer.

00:27:35.970 --> 00:27:39.030
And so this is just a more
flexible load balancer

00:27:39.030 --> 00:27:42.183
that doesn't really care about
high level protocols like HTTP.

00:27:42.183 --> 00:27:44.850
So this could be used
for any sort of protocol

00:27:44.850 --> 00:27:46.514
that happens over TCP.

00:27:46.514 --> 00:27:47.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:27:47.430 --> 00:27:52.370
So basically, if you move
to the TCP load balancer,

00:27:52.370 --> 00:27:58.130
you will be losing the fact that
you have global load balancing,

00:27:58.130 --> 00:28:00.490
because the TCP load
balancer is actually

00:28:00.490 --> 00:28:04.970
regional, which in many
cases, actually, totally fine.

00:28:04.970 --> 00:28:07.950
So if that's something that
you're fine with, then,

00:28:07.950 --> 00:28:09.610
yeah, just use the
TCP load balancer,

00:28:09.610 --> 00:28:11.710
and WebSockets are
completely supported.

00:28:11.710 --> 00:28:14.570
But if not, we have this
different load balancer,

00:28:14.570 --> 00:28:19.030
which is the SSL load balancer,
that manages SSL, obviously.

00:28:19.030 --> 00:28:20.300
That is the name.

00:28:20.300 --> 00:28:25.520
But also, it does routing
across different regions.

00:28:25.520 --> 00:28:29.484
So again, if a WebSocket
started from Barcelona,

00:28:29.484 --> 00:28:31.900
it would go to Belgium, but
if it goes from San Francisco,

00:28:31.900 --> 00:28:35.280
you'd go to, I think it's Oregon
or something, like West Coast.

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:37.030
CHRIS BROADFOOT: And
I guess that's pretty

00:28:37.030 --> 00:28:39.030
important for WebSockets
because they're usually

00:28:39.030 --> 00:28:41.580
like low latency kind
of thing, maybe a game,

00:28:41.580 --> 00:28:43.687
like an interactive
real-time game or something.

00:28:43.687 --> 00:28:45.520
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If
you're using WebSockets,

00:28:45.520 --> 00:28:47.690
you're basically
trying to do real time.

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:50.820
So if you end up in a
different continent,

00:28:50.820 --> 00:28:53.270
yeah, the real times aren't
going to be that real.

00:28:53.270 --> 00:28:56.106
So yeah, so check
it out, so we're

00:28:56.106 --> 00:28:57.730
going to have links
from the show notes

00:28:57.730 --> 00:29:00.420
to all the documentation
to both the TCP load

00:29:00.420 --> 00:29:02.530
balancer, the HTTPS
load balancer,

00:29:02.530 --> 00:29:04.290
and then finally, the
SSL load balancer.

00:29:04.290 --> 00:29:06.248
CHRIS BROADFOOT: And all
of the PHP stuff, too.

00:29:06.248 --> 00:29:08.910
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And all
the PHP stuff, lots of links

